# Core engineering rules

- Read the relevant code, tests, configuration, and documentation before editing.
- For non-trivial work, produce an implementation plan before changing code.
- Follow existing repository conventions unless the task explicitly requires changing them.
- Prefer the smallest coherent diff that solves the root problem.
- Do not rewrite unrelated code, generated files, lockfiles, or formatting across the repository.
- Preserve public APIs, data contracts, and backwards compatibility unless a breaking change is approved.
- Never hardcode secrets, credentials, private keys, production URLs, or tokens.
- Never run destructive production, database, infrastructure, or deployment commands without explicit user approval.
- Do not claim a command, test, build, migration, or deployment succeeded unless it was actually run and its result inspected.
- Add or update tests for behavior changes and regressions.
- Handle errors explicitly; do not swallow exceptions or return ambiguous failure states.
- Reuse existing abstractions when they fit. Do not create speculative frameworks or premature generalization.
- Keep changes reviewable. Split large tasks into logical steps and report material risks early.
- At completion report: changed areas, verification performed, remaining risks, and follow-up work.
